// A FUNCTION DEFINED IN ONE REGION, CALLED FROM A REGION NESTED INSIDE IT.
//
// `mk` is written inside `region R`, so the container it builds is typed `Vec[R, int]`
// and `cache`, also in R, can hold it: the types are consistent and the escape check is
// right to say nothing. What was wrong was the ARENA. `mk` is lifted to its own
// function, so the block's `__region_R` local does not reach it, and the backend fell
// back on the RUNTIME CURRENT REGION -- which at the call is S, not R. The value went
// into S's arena, S ended, T reused it, and the read came back as T's filler.
//
//   interp   1 / 700          (no arenas, so nothing to get wrong)
//   C        4096 / -1        (before v0.1.454)
//
// The fix is the same shape as Q-127's: the region travels as a VALUE. A lifted body
// takes the region blocks it was written inside as captures, like any other free
// variable, so `mk` allocates in R wherever it is called from.
//
// T's filler is 4096 pushes because the arena is REUSED, not freed: without something
// written over it the stale bytes read back correctly and the bug looks absent.
// A sanitiser cannot see it either -- the arena is one live allocation.
// The filler is a TOP-LEVEL `let rec` so the LLVM backend can take this file too --
// it refuses a `let rec` inside an expression, and this case is about a hole that
// backend has as well.
let rec fill = fn (j) -> fn (i: int) ->
  if i >= 4096 then 0 else let _ = vec_push j (0 - 1) in fill j (i + 1);

let _ = region R {
  let mk = fn (n: int) -> let v = vec_new () in let _ = vec_push v n in v in
  let cache = vec_new () in
  let _ = region S { vec_push cache (mk 700) } in
  let _ = region T { let junk = vec_new () in fill junk 0 } in
  let got = vec_get cache 0 in
  let _ = print_int (vec_len got) in
  print_int (vec_get got 0)
};
0
